    "result": "I've successfully fixed the bug in the date calculation for invoices with 'eom' (end of month) payment terms issued in February of a leap year.\n\n## Problem Analysis\nThe issue was in the `end_of_month` function in `invoicing/dates.py`. The original implementation used a fixed array `_DAYS = [31, 28, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31]` that always used 28 days for February, regardless of whether the year was a leap year.\n\n## Solution\nI modified the `end_of_month` function to:\n1. Properly detect leap years using `calendar.isleap()`\n2. Return 29 days for February in leap years and 28 days in non-leap years\n3. Use `calendar.monthrange()` for other months to ensure accuracy\n\n## Verification\n- The specific failing tests now pass:\n  - `test_end_of_month_leap_february` \n  - `test_eom_terms_in_leap_year`\n- All 85 tests in the test suite pass\n- The fix works for any year, not just 2024\n- February 2024 (leap year) correctly returns 29 days\n- February 2023 (non-leap year) correctly returns 28 days\n- All other months continue to work as expected\n\nThe fix is general and handles leap years correctly for all invoice dates with EOM payment terms, resolving the issue described in the task.",
